Aerie-Purpose: Give WebGL readPixels from the default framebuffer (RGBA, UNSIGNED_BYTE) the same per-site, per-session canvas noise as getImageData, and leave reads from framebuffer objects and other formats exact (issue #119).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc (ReadPixelsHelper), third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/BUILD.gn, third_party/blink/renderer/core/helium_noise/canvas_noising_helper.{h,cc}
Aerie-Kind: hook

Amends helium-chromium/patches/helium/core/noise/canvas.patch (issue #119, part of #87). Helium noises
2D canvas readback (getImageData, toBlob, toDataURL) and nothing in WebGL, so readPixels on a WebGL
canvas gave the real pixels, which differ by driver and GPU.

ReadPixelsHelper decides with aerie_webgl_readback::ShouldNoiseReadPixels(!framebuffer, format, type),
which is true only for the default framebuffer in RGBA/UNSIGNED_BYTE, and then calls the new
CanvasNoisingHelper::MaybeNoiseReadPixels. That function takes the kCanvas token through
GetCanvasTokenHash, the same gate as getImageData: it does nothing without the token (protection off
for the browser or for the site, or kHeliumNoiseCanvas disabled) and without kHeliumNoiseCanvas, so the
real bytes come back. The token is derived per site and session in the browser and the renderer never
sees the session token; a worker (OffscreenCanvas) uses its own tokens (issue #113). The noise is
keyed on the pixel position in the framebuffer, so two reads of overlapping areas agree. Which reads
count and where the pixels lie in the destination (PACK_ROW_LENGTH, PACK_SKIP_PIXELS, PACK_SKIP_ROWS,
PACK_ALIGNMENT) are pure code with unit tests in overlay/components/aerie_webgl_readback. A read with
padded rows is noised on a tightly packed copy and copied back, so the padding bytes are not touched.

Brave (checked in brave-core): it does not noise readPixels. Its Maximum (strict) level makes
ReadPixelsHelper return early, so the destination buffer is left unwritten, and its other levels leave
the call alone. Aerie noises the default framebuffer instead, so scripts still get a usable image.

Not changed: readPixels from framebuffer objects, float and other formats, into a pixel pack buffer,
and WebGPU readback. Limits: the bytes are noised after the GL call returns, so a call that wrote
nothing (a GL error, or pixels outside the framebuffer) still gets noise on the bytes the script
passed in, which is at most one level on a channel.

